; bale-crier changedetection timer — user-level. See bale-crier-changedetection.service for
; the install notes.
;
; This timer, not the service, is the unit to enable:
;   systemctl --user enable --now bale-crier-changedetection.timer
;
; Every three hours at half past (00:30, 03:30, … 21:30), half an hour after
; changedetection.io fetches its pages. changedetection keeps a *per-watch* phase — each
; page is fetched three hours after its own last fetch — so "half past" is only half an
; hour after it because those phases were aligned to the hour once, by forcing a recheck
; of every active watch at 18:00 on 2026-10-21. Reading before changedetection has fetched
; would report last round's change, and reading hours later would deliver it hours late.
;
; Three hours, not less: that is how often changedetection itself looks at a page here
; (its global `time_between_check`), so a faster read would cost a request and tell the
; owner nothing sooner. If changedetection's phases drift over the months, re-align them
; by forcing a recheck of every watch at the top of the hour.
;
; The daily bale-crier.timer does not run this watcher — it owns this timer — so the two
; timers cannot race over the watermarks.

[Unit]
Description=Check changedetection.io for changed pages every three hours, half past

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 00/3:30:00
Persistent=true
RandomizedDelaySec=2m

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
